The actual total-degree pair and actual partial-y-degree pair in the original (x,y) coordinates are both (99,66), because the fixed monic tops attain them. Coefficient-x support is finite, bounded by the total-degree caps. The filtration below uses ordinary weights (1,1) in (Y,W); it introduces no new weighted-degree or coefficient-height cap. This is the packet's still-unresolved fixed family, not an enlargement of the classical low-degree search window.

Set Y=y and W=y−x, so x=Y−W; this coordinate change has determinant +1. In these coordinates h_0=Y^9 W^24. Write

    J(F,G) = B(h,D,a,b) + L(C),
    B = ((3D+a+b h)/2) J(h,D),
    L(C) = (2h−b/3) J(C,h) + J(C,D).

This is the charged chain-rule identity, just separated into its affine C part. For a homogeneous C_r of total degree r, the degree r+64 part of L(C_r) is

    T_r(C_r) = 2 h_0 J(C_r,h_0).

All other terms of L(C_r) have smaller degree. More explicitly,

    T_r(Y^i W^j) = 2(24i−9j) Y^(i+17) W^(j+47),  i+j=r.

Distinct monomials have distinct images. Thus every nonzero diagonal entry is a nonzero rational constant, independent of every coefficient parameter. Its zero entries are precisely (i,j)=(3k,8k). Through r=35 the kernel monomials are 1, Y^3W^8, Y^6W^16, Y^9W^24, at degrees 0,11,22,33. No centralizer theorem or generic-field division is needed for this calculation.

